# Coverage gate configuration -- the single place to edit when ratcheting.
#
# BASELINE_* is what CI enforces today. It is set to the coverage this repo
# actually has, not to the coverage it wants, so the gate turns green honestly
# while still failing the build the moment a change drops coverage. A gate
# pinned to an aspirational number has to be left disabled, which is exactly
# how this repo ended up reporting "Failing" on every passing build.
#
# TARGET_* is the long-term goal. It is never enforced; it is printed beside
# the baseline in the PR comment so the remaining gap stays visible instead of
# quietly disappearing once the build is green.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# How these numbers are derived:  BASELINE = floor(min(observed) - 1.0)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# `min(observed)` spans BOTH environments -- local macOS and Linux CI -- not
# just the one you happen to be sitting in front of. Two independent effects
# make a single-run baseline wrong, and the first release of this gate got
# caught by both (see issue #14):
#
# 1. Run-to-run drift. Coverage moves without any source change, because line
#    execution inside passing tests depends on async ordering and timing. Domain
#    was observed at 54.0 and 53.1 on the same machine with identical test
#    outcomes (1310 passed, 572 skipped both times) -- 0.9 points of drift. A
#    baseline set from one run left 0.1 points of headroom and would have turned
#    main red for nobody's mistake.
#
# 2. Platform-level denominator differences. Flutter only instruments libraries
#    the test run loads, and the loaded set is not identical across platforms.
#    lib/shared/widgets/language_switcher.dart (98 lines, 0% covered) is in the
#    local lcov and absent from CI's, which alone moves `shared` from 47.8%
#    local to 60.2% on CI. Taking the *lower* environment keeps the gate usable
#    in both places; a CI-derived 59 here would fail every local run, and a gate
#    developers learn to ignore is the disease this whole thing is treating.
#    Issue #15 removes that divergence, after which `shared` can be ratcheted
#    toward CI's real number.
#
# Recorded observations behind the values below:
#
#   layer         local           CI              min     baseline
#   total         32.3, 32.4      32.5, 32.6      32.3    31
#   domain        53.1, 54.0      53.1, 53.5      53.1    52
#   data          18.9            18.9            18.9    17
#   presentation  37.0            37.0            37.0    36
#   core          14.0            14.0            14.0    13
#   shared        47.8            60.2            47.8    46
#
# Ratcheting: after tests land and BOTH environments report a higher number,
# raise the matching BASELINE_* here. Nothing else needs to change. Never lower
# a baseline to make a red build green -- that is the regression the gate exists
# to catch. Lowering is legitimate only when a measurement changes meaning, as
# in #15, and then it belongs in that change with the reason recorded.

BASELINE_TOTAL=31
BASELINE_DOMAIN=52
BASELINE_DATA=17
BASELINE_PRESENTATION=36
BASELINE_CORE=13
BASELINE_SHARED=46

TARGET_TOTAL=80
TARGET_DOMAIN=100
TARGET_DATA=90
TARGET_PRESENTATION=80
TARGET_CORE=80
TARGET_SHARED=80
